CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*does not uniquely resolve.*$
--
Verifies that [temp.deduct.partial] forwarding-reference partial
ordering correctly prefers the lvalue-reference overload over a
forwarding-reference (rvalue-reference) overload when the call
argument is an lvalue.  This is the libstdc++ idiom of using
`T&& = delete` to forbid xvalue arguments to `std::as_const`,
`std::ranges::ref_view`, etc.  Without partial ordering, both
overloads are equally viable for an lvalue argument (both deduce
`T&` parameter type after reference collapsing) and the resolve
returns
  symbol 'as_const' does not uniquely resolve
breaking every TU that includes `<utility>`.
